# Python Production Standards

- Use typed configuration validated at startup; read secrets from runtime configuration only.
- Keep functions focused and make business rules pure where practical.
- Isolate database and network access behind explicit interfaces.
- Use context managers for connections, cursors, files, and transactions.
- Use fixed-precision decimal values for money; do not use floats.
- Raise domain-specific exceptions with preserved causes; never use empty or broad silent catches.
- Emit structured logs with run/source context and without credentials or customer PII.
- Make retries bounded and limited to transient failures; never retry deterministic validation errors.
- Make write operations idempotent and transaction boundaries explicit.
- Validate inputs at system boundaries and fail closed on incompatible schemas.
- Add unit tests for normal, boundary, invalid, and retry behavior.
- Avoid global mutable state, import-time I/O, and hardcoded local paths.
- CLI modules parse arguments and call application services; they do not implement transformations.
